# Karna — Personal AI Assistant

## Overview
Karna is a personal AI assistant deployed on Cloudflare Pages + D1 (SQLite). It runs entirely serverless at the edge. Users interact via a web UI or Telegram. The agent uses a rotating pool of LLM providers and has access to a suite of tools for Google Workspace, web research, scheduling, and more.

**Production URL:** https://karna-5xs.pages.dev
**GitHub:** https://github.com/ashwinjyoti-ship-it/personal-ai-assistant
**Stack:** Hono (routing), Cloudflare Pages (hosting), Cloudflare D1 (database), Vite (build)
**Version:** v4.2.1

---

## Architecture

```
src/
  index.tsx                 — App entry point, route mounting
  types/index.ts            — Shared types, LLM provider registry
  frontend.ts               — Single-file web UI (vanilla JS, no framework)
  routes/
    auth.ts                 — PIN-based auth, session management, Google OAuth
    chat.ts                 — Messaging, threads, streaming SSE, dashboard, notifications
    settings.ts             — Profile, credentials, memory, schedules, Google integration
    system.ts               — Health, cron execution engine, status, tool_execution_log queries
    proactive.ts            — Evening briefings, briefing preferences
    channels/
      telegram.ts           — Telegram webhook handler (text + voice notes)
      adapter.ts            — Message normalisation across channels
  services/
    agent.ts                — Tool-calling agent loop (runAgent / runSubAgent) + enforcement
    router.ts               — Intent classification + sub-agent prompt builder
    briefing.ts             — Evening briefing generation (calendar, email, news, tasks)
    memory.ts               — Semantic memory store/search
    research.ts             — Multi-step web research
    crypto.ts               — AES-GCM credential encryption
    gmail.ts                — Gmail API service (list, read, search, send, draft w/ CC, modify)
    google.ts               — Google OAuth token management
    google-apis.ts          — Sheets, Calendar, Drive, Docs helpers
    llm/provider.ts         — Multi-provider LLM abstraction + rotation + fallback + credit alerts
migrations/                 — D1 SQL migrations (0001–0011)
```
- **Cron**: in-process on Render (`src/render/cron.ts`, every 60s) — no external cron-job.org / cron-worker dependency.

---

## Authentication
- PIN-based login (no email/password)
- Session tokens stored in D1, expire after 30 days
- Google OAuth 2.0 for Workspace integrations (stored as encrypted refresh token)
- All credentials encrypted with AES-GCM using PIN hash as key

---

## LLM Providers
Up to 3 concurrent provider slots. Auto-fallback if a provider returns auth/billing errors.

| Provider | Default Model |
|---|---|
| Anthropic Claude | claude-sonnet-5 |
| OpenAI GPT | gpt-4o |
| xAI Grok | grok-3-mini |
| DeepSeek | deepseek-chat |
| Google Gemini | gemini-2.0-flash |
| OpenRouter | anthropic/claude-sonnet-5 (configurable) |
| Abacus AI RouteLLM | route-llm (auto-routing) |

Model can be overridden per slot. Provider rotation is in-memory (no DB tracking).

**Credit/Auth Alert System:** When an LLM provider returns 401/403/429 errors, the system detects it, applies a cooldown (1440 min for auth, 10 min for rate-limit, 5 min otherwise), and sends a Telegram alert to the user.

---

## Sub-Agent Router (`services/router.ts`)

Intent classification uses a two-tier system:
1. **Keyword heuristics** — regex-based patterns, runs in <5ms, handles ~80% of queries
2. **LLM fallback** — used only when heuristics are ambiguous

### Agent Types
| Agent | Scope |
|---|---|
| `scheduler` | Schedules, reminders, timers |
| `workspace` | Google Sheets, Docs, Drive, Calendar, Gmail |
| `research` | Web search, URL reading, deep research |
| `memory` | Store/recall memory, system status |
| `conversation` | General chat, creative, personality |
| `multi` | Multi-intent — needs full agent (fallback) |

Each sub-agent gets a **focused system prompt** (from `buildSubAgentPrompt`) and only the tools relevant to its scope (from `getToolsForAgent`).

---

## Agent Tools (35+)

### Scheduling
- `create_schedule` — Create a recurring or one-off task (`once`, `interval`, `daily`, `weekly`, timezone-aware)
- `list_schedules` — List all scheduled tasks
- `toggle_schedule` — Enable/disable a schedule
- `update_schedule_state` — Update schedule execution state
- `delete_schedule` — Remove a schedule

### Memory
- `store_memory` — Save a fact/preference to long-term memory
- `search_memory` — Semantic search over stored memories

### Google Sheets
- `read_sheet` — Read data from a spreadsheet range (returns all tab names)
- `write_sheet` — Write/overwrite data to a range (**auto-pads rows with empty strings to clear stale columns**)
- `append_sheet` — Append rows to a sheet
- `create_sheet` — Create a new spreadsheet (optionally in a Drive folder)

### Google Calendar
- `list_calendar_events` — List events in a date range
- `create_calendar_event` — Create an event with title, time, description, attendees

### Google Docs
- `create_doc` — Create a new Google Doc (optionally in a Drive folder)
- `read_doc` — Read content of an existing Doc
- `append_to_doc` — Append text to an existing Doc

### Gmail
- `gmail_list` — List recent emails
- `gmail_read` — Read a specific email by ID
- `gmail_search` — Search emails by query
- `gmail_send` — Send an email (with CC/BCC support)
- `gmail_draft` — Save a draft email (**with CC support** — added v4.1.0)
- `gmail_unread_count` — Get unread email count
- `gmail_modify` — Archive, trash, star, mark read/unread

### Google Drive
- `drive_list` — List files in Drive or a folder
- `drive_search` — Search Drive by filename/content

### Web & Research
- `web_search` — Google Custom Search API
- `read_url` — Fetch and parse a web page
- `research` — Multi-step deep research (Exa search/content retrieval + Sonnet 5 planning/synthesis, auto-escalating to Opus 4.8 only if a Sonnet call fails or returns empty; falls back to DuckDuckGo + LLM synthesis if no Exa key is configured)

### Places & Maps
- `search_places` — Search nearby places (Google Places API)
- `get_place_details` — Get details for a specific place
- `get_directions` — Get directions between two points
- `get_travel_time` — Estimate travel time with traffic

### Utilities
- `translate_text` — Translate text via Google Translate API
- `search_youtube` — Search YouTube videos
- `geocode_address` — Convert address to coordinates
- `get_system_status` — Report active schedules, memory count, message count

---

## Channels

### Web UI
- Vanilla JS rendering (`src/frontend.ts`) via string templates
- Streaming chat via SSE (`/api/chat/stream`)
- Thread management (create, rename, delete, archive)
- Dashboard: thread count, memory count, active schedules, recent threads, unread notifications
- Notifications panel
- Settings: profile, API keys, memory manager, schedule manager, Google integration, error log

### Telegram
- Webhook-based message handling
- Full agent tool access (same as web)
- Supports voice notes (audio extraction + transcription)
- Commands: `/start`, `/status`, `/schedules`, `/memory`, `/clear`, `/briefing`

---

## Scheduling Engine
- Cron jobs stored in D1 (`cron_jobs` table)
- External cron worker calls `/api/system/cron/execute` every minute
- Schedule types: `once` (single run), `interval` (every N minutes), `daily` (HH:MM), `weekly` (Weekday HH:MM in user's timezone)
- Overlap prevention via `cron_execution_log` lock table
- Job results delivered via Telegram notification

---

## Evening Briefings
Configurable daily briefing generated by the agent, delivered via Telegram and/or web:
- Tomorrow's Google Calendar events
- Gmail unread summary (top senders, subjects)
- Pending tasks from memory
- News by custom topics (via web search)
- Configurable delivery time (default 20:00 in user's timezone)

---

## Database Schema (D1 Tables)
- `users`: Core user accounts
- `sessions`: Auth sessions
- `credentials`: Encrypted API keys & tokens
- `conversations` / `threads`: Chat history
- `memory`: Long-term fact storage
- `cron_jobs` / `cron_execution_log`: Scheduling engine
- `notifications`: Web UI alerts
- `error_log`: System error tracking
- `briefings`: Evening briefing history
- `briefing_preferences`: User configuration for proactive briefings
- `tool_execution_log`: Audit trail for all tool executions (tool name, args, result, latency, enforcement flags)

---

## Integrity Layer — Tool Enforcement & Anti-Fabrication System

### The Problem (discovered v4.0.1 testing)
LLMs frequently **narrate** actions instead of **executing** them. Observed failure modes:

1. **Phantom writes**: LLM says "Fixed all rows!" but never called `write_sheet`. Response includes fake `[TOOLS_USED: read_sheet, write_sheet]` tag copied from conversation history.
2. **Read-only loops**: LLM calls `read_sheet`, sees the data, describes what it would fix, but stops. Enforcement triggers retry, which also only reads.
3. **Poisoned history**: Fabricated responses enter conversation history. Future turns treat them as ground truth, compounding errors (e.g., "9 Mar" date from a hallucinated entry propagated across 4 subsequent turns).
4. **Column spillover**: `write_sheet` only overwrites columns it's given data for. Extra columns (E, F, etc.) with stale data persist silently.
5. **Fake audit tags**: The LLM generates `[TOOLS_USED: ...]` text in its response by copying patterns from earlier messages, bypassing the system's real tool-tracking.
6. **Placeholder emails**: LLM invents email addresses (name@example.com) instead of searching Gmail for real contacts.

### The Fixes (v4.1.0 – v4.2.0)

#### 1. Tool Enforcement Loop (`agent.ts` ~line 2381)
For agents that MUST use tools (`scheduler`, `workspace`, `research`):
- After the sub-agent loop completes, check if required tools were actually called
- **Scheduler check**: If user asked for a reminder but `create_schedule` wasn't called → enforce
- **Workspace check**: If user asked to fix/update and `read_sheet` was called but `write_sheet` wasn't → enforce
- Enforcement injects a `[SYSTEM OVERRIDE]` message and runs a **mini agentic loop** (up to 5 turns) allowing the LLM to chain read → write → verify
- Scheduler has a **programmatic fallback** (`parseReminderFromText`) that creates the schedule directly if the LLM still refuses

#### 2. Fake [TOOLS_USED:] Stripping (`agent.ts` ~line 2544)
- Before storing any assistant response, strip any `[TOOLS_USED: ...]` prefix the LLM may have generated
- The system adds its own verified `toolEvidence` tag based on actual `toolsCalledList`
- Prevents future context poisoning from self-reported tool usage

#### 3. Auto-Pad + Clear Stale Columns (`agent.ts` write_sheet handler ~line 1170)
- Every `write_sheet` call now auto-pads rows with 4 extra empty-string columns
- Expands the write range (e.g., A1:D8 → A1:H8) to overwrite any spillover data
- Prevents stale data in columns beyond the intended range

#### 4. Server-Side Date Injection (`router.ts` ~line 248)
- `buildSubAgentPrompt` now injects `Today's date for sheets` (e.g., "8 Mar 2026") into the user block
- Workspace prompt explicitly says: "use the date from 'Today's date for sheets', NEVER copy dates from conversation history"
- Matches existing sheet date formats using `Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB')`

#### 5. Mandatory Verify-After-Write (workspace prompt rule 6)
- Workspace sub-agent prompt includes: "VERIFY after writes. When you use write_sheet, call read_sheet afterward to confirm the data landed correctly."
- Rule 5: "NEVER claim you fixed data based on a previous conversation's tool results. Every new turn must call tools itself."

#### 6. gmail_draft CC Support (`gmail.ts` + `agent.ts`)
- `gmail_draft` tool now accepts a `cc` parameter
- `createDraft` method builds proper RFC 2822 `Cc:` header
- Workspace prompt includes "NEVER put Cc: in the body text"

#### 7. Mandatory Recipient Lookup (workspace prompt)
- "When drafting/sending, you MUST call gmail_search FIRST to find real addresses"
- "NEVER use placeholder addresses like name@example.com"

### What the Enforcement Does NOT Catch (Known Gaps)
- **append_sheet enforcement**: Only `write_sheet` absence triggers enforcement. If user says "add Coffee 350" and LLM reads but doesn't append, the regex check (`/fix|correct|update.../`) may not match "add". Mitigation: the prompt's read-before-append rule should handle this, but it's not enforced programmatically.
- **Multi-tab selection**: LLM may write to wrong tab. No programmatic check exists.
- **Concurrent writes**: Two rapid requests could both read the same row count and produce conflicting appends. D1 has no row-level locking.
- **Long-context degradation**: After ~25+ messages, the LLM may ignore system rules. No context window pressure relief beyond the existing compaction (keep last 30 messages).

---

## Testing Results (v4.2.0 — March 2026)

### What Works Reliably
- **Single-intent tool execution**: Gmail send/draft/search, Sheets read, Calendar list, web search, memory store/recall — all execute correctly on first attempt
- **Multi-intent routing**: Scheduler + research requests are classified and routed correctly
- **Cron/scheduled tasks**: Execute on time, Telegram delivery works, honesty rule prevents fabricated cron responses
- **Error-based LLM credit alerts**: Provider auth/billing failures detected, cooldown applied, Telegram notification sent
- **Enforcement retry loop**: The 5-turn mini agentic loop now correctly chains read → write → verify
- **Auto-column cleanup**: Stale columns beyond write range are automatically cleared

### Works With Guardrails
- **Sheet writes** — Forced verify-after-write catches most silent failures
- **Column cleanup** — Auto-pad prevents spillover persistence
- **Date accuracy** — Server-side injection prevents hallucinated dates
- **Email CC** — gmail_draft now produces proper RFC 2822 headers
- **Fake audit trail removal** — System-verified tool tags replace LLM self-reports
- **Recipient lookup** — Prompt rule prevents placeholder emails (needs more enforcement)

### Still Fragile / Untested
- **Tier 2 daily brief cron**: Scheduler agent didn't create the email-schedule when asked for a cross-service daily brief (calendar → sheet → compose → send every morning). Needs explicit multi-step cron support.
- **Outlook integration**: Only Gmail is natively integrated. Outlook requires browser automation (Steel.dev/Browser Use Cloud) which is slow and unreliable.
- **Multi-tab sheet operations**: LLM may read tab names but select the wrong one.
- **Concurrent sheet modifications**: No row-level locking in D1 → potential duplicate rows on rapid sequential requests.
- **Long-context degradation**: After ~25 messages in a single thread, system rules get diluted.
- **append_sheet enforcement**: No programmatic enforcement — relies purely on prompt rules.

---

## Conversation History Hygiene

### Compaction
- `memory.compactHistory(userId, 30)` — keeps last 30 messages per user
- Older messages are discarded (not summarised)

### History Stripping in Sub-Agents
- Recent conversations loaded via `memory.getRecentConversations(userId, 25)`
- `[TOOLS_USED: ...]` tags are stripped from history before building LLM context (prevents the model from learning narration patterns)

### Poisoned History Cleanup
- Direct D1 updates used to annotate/correct fabricated conversation entries
- No automated detection — manual intervention required when a fabricated response is identified

